# Host-side bind mount paths — set to wherever you store Docker data
DATA_DB_PATH=/your/host/path/db
DATA_UPLOADS_PATH=/your/host/path/uploads

# Required if using user management — use a long random string, keep it secret
# Generate one: openssl rand -base64 48
JWT_SECRET=change-me-to-a-long-random-secret
# Or mount it as a Docker/Swarm secret:
# JWT_SECRET_FILE=/run/secrets/lifttrace_jwt_secret

# Optional — At-rest encryption key for OIDC client secrets and wearable
# OAuth tokens. Defaults to a key derived from JWT_SECRET, which means
# rotating JWT_SECRET also invalidates every encrypted secret. Set
# TOKEN_ENC_KEY explicitly if you want to rotate session tokens
# independently of stored secrets.
# Generate one: openssl rand -base64 48
# TOKEN_ENC_KEY=change-me-to-a-long-random-key
# TOKEN_ENC_KEY_FILE=/run/secrets/lifttrace_token_enc_key

# Optional: lockout recovery token
# Required for Locked Out? on the login page: reset two-step sign-in for one
# account (16 characters or more) or disable user management
# Without this, the recovery endpoint is disabled for safety
# RECOVERY_TOKEN=my-secret-recovery-phrase
# RECOVERY_TOKEN_FILE=/run/secrets/lifttrace_recovery_token
# Optional: the key that locks two-step sign-in secrets. Unset, LiftTrace
# makes two-step.key next to the database and keeps it. Set it only to keep
# it outside the data folder; changing it later stops authenticator codes
# (recovery codes still work) until two-step sign-in is reset.
# TWO_STEP_KEY=change-me-to-a-long-random-key
# TWO_STEP_KEY_FILE=/run/secrets/lifttrace_two_step_key

# Optional: Sign in over plain HTTP (no TLS in front)
# The sign-in cookie is HTTPS-only by default (marked Secure), so a browser
# keeps it only on an https:// page (Chrome and Firefox also on
# http://localhost). Opened at a plain http:// address (a LAN IP, an Unraid
# WebUI link), signing in sends you straight back to the sign-in page: the
# password was right, but the browser threw the cookie away. The sign-in and setup screens say so. Set this to 1
# to send the cookie over plain HTTP too. It then travels in cleartext, so
# only do this on a trusted home network, and leave it unset (0) behind HTTPS
# (a reverse proxy with TLS, Cloudflare Tunnel, Tailscale Serve). The Android
# app signs in with a token, not this cookie.
# More: https://traceapps.github.io/docs/getting-started/lan-http/
# INSECURE_COOKIES=1

# Optional: the server's time zone (default UTC). Backup file names, and a backup
# schedule with no time zone of its own (BACKUP_TIME included), use it. Set it
# here, not inside a running container, then restart; the image needs no tzdata.
# TZ=America/New_York

# Optional: addresses allowed to show LiftTrace inside a frame. By default only
# LiftTrace itself may, so no other site can frame it to trick a click. If you
# show LiftTrace inside a dashboard (Homarr, Organizr, Home Assistant), set the
# dashboard's address here or it shows blank. Origins only; a path is ignored.
# Several separated by spaces or commas; https://*.example.com covers a domain.
# FRAME_ANCESTORS=https://home.example.com

# Optional — Mount the app at a path other than root (for reverse-proxy setups)
# Example: BASE_URL=/lifttrace will serve the app at https://example.com/lifttrace/
# Default unset = served at root path. Configure your reverse proxy to pass the
# path through *without* stripping it.
# BASE_URL=/lifttrace

# Optional: The full address people open the app at, subpath included
# Links in password reset, invite, coaching and weekly summary emails use it.
# Unset, they use an address an admin has opened the app at; until an admin
# has opened it once, only an admin's own request sends an email. Never taken
# from request headers, so a forged Host can't point a reset link at someone
# else's site. The server also takes it as the app's own address when it
# refuses changes sent from other sites' pages: set it if your reverse proxy
# passes neither the public Host nor X-Forwarded-Host; otherwise changes from
# a browser are refused (403, cross_site) over plain http, and over https from
# older browsers without Sec-Fetch-Site (such as Safari before 16.4).
# PUBLIC_URL=https://lift.example.com

# Optional — Log level (error | warn | info | debug)
# Default: info
# LOG_LEVEL=info

# Optional — SMTP email (for password reset & user invites)
# If set, these override whatever is configured in the Settings UI AND lock those fields for all users
# SMTP_HOST=smtp.example.com
# SMTP_PORT=587
# SMTP_SECURE=false        # true for port 465 (SSL), false for STARTTLS
# SMTP_USER=you@example.com
# SMTP_PASS=your-password
# SMTP_FROM=LiftTrace <noreply@example.com>
# SMTP_PASS_FILE=/run/secrets/lifttrace_smtp_pass

# Optional — Trace AI (shared key for all users)
# If set, AI calls are proxied through the server (key never reaches the browser)
# and the provider/model/key fields are locked in Settings for all users.
# Supports every provider the app supports — cloud (claude / openai / gemini)
# and OpenAI-compatible local endpoints (Ollama, LM Studio, LocalAI, vLLM,
# DeepSeek, Groq, etc.). Set AI_PROVIDER=oai-compat + AI_BASE_URL + AI_MODEL
# to point at a private-network LLM the browser can't reach directly (e.g.
# a Docker Compose sidecar on an internal network).
# AI_PROVIDER=claude        # claude | openai | gemini | oai-compat
# AI_API_KEY=sk-ant-...      # required for cloud providers; optional for oai-compat
# AI_API_KEY_FILE=/run/secrets/lifttrace_ai_api_key
# AI_MODEL=claude-haiku-5-5            # optional for cloud (defaults to provider's fast model); REQUIRED for oai-compat
# AI_BASE_URL=http://ollama:11434       # REQUIRED when AI_PROVIDER=oai-compat; with or without /v1; must be reachable from the server container
# AI_ENABLED=true           # optional, auto-enables Trace for all users

# Optional — OIDC Single Sign-On (declared in env instead of the Settings UI)
#
# Single-provider shorthand — `OIDC_*` is an alias for `OIDC_PROVIDER_1_*`:
# OIDC_ISSUER=https://auth.example.com
# OIDC_CLIENT_ID=lifttrace
# OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=...
# OIDC_DISPLAY_NAME=Authentik
# OIDC_REDIRECT_URIS=https://lifttrace.example.com/api/auth/oidc/callback/1     # comma-separated for multi
# OIDC_SCOPE=openid profile email
# OIDC_TOKEN_AUTH_METHOD=client_secret_post   # or client_secret_basic | none
# OIDC_ADMIN_GROUP_CLAIM=groups
# OIDC_ADMIN_GROUP_VALUE=LiftTraceAdmins
# OIDC_AUTO_LINK=1                            # link verified-email to existing users (default 1)
# OIDC_AUTO_REGISTER=0                        # blanket signup on first SSO login (default 0)
# OIDC_IS_ACTIVE=1
#
# Android builds from before the one-time sign-in code take their sign-in in
# the SSO deep link, which another app could catch. They still sign in (said
# in the log each time) unless this is off; off will become the default.
# OIDC_APP_LINK_TOKEN=off
#
# Multi-provider — use the numbered prefix:
# OIDC_PROVIDER_2_ISSUER=https://other-idp.example.com
# OIDC_PROVIDER_2_CLIENT_ID=...
# OIDC_PROVIDER_2_CLIENT_SECRET=...
# OIDC_PROVIDER_2_DISPLAY_NAME=Keycloak
#
# Env-defined providers show with a lock badge in Settings → Admin → OIDC
# providers and are read-only. Edit via .env / docker-compose / k8s
# secrets — UI mutations on env-defined providers are refused server-side.

# ── Radio stations on your own network ───────────────────────────────────
# The radio proxy fetches station addresses from this server. An admin may
# play stations on the server's own network; other accounts may not, so
# they can't read services only the server can see. Set this to let every
# account (and a single-user install, which has no sign-in). Cloud-metadata
# addresses stay blocked. Behind HTTP_PROXY/HTTPS_PROXY the proxy connects.
# ALLOW_PRIVATE_RADIO_URLS=1

# ── Update checks ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Off until you turn them on: setup asks, and Settings, Updates can change it.
# When on, the server asks github.com for the latest release every few hours
# and shows a banner when one is out. Nothing about you or your data is sent.
# Set this to off to keep them off for good, whatever the setting says.
# UPDATE_CHECK=off
